On 18 Dec 2006 18:20:33 -0800, "Malcolm Ryan" <malcolm.ryan@gmail.com>
wrote:

> > Focusing on "property rights" here confuses the issue.  Nethack is an
> > open source game, in practice enthusiastically developed for by an
> > active fan base.  I would venture that significantly more time and
> > work has gone into unofficial nethack coding that that produced by the
> > moribund devteam - and certainly more results are published per unit
> > time, much of it high quality.  By several measurements, the devteam
> > does a poor job of maintaining the game, specifically considering the
> > high interest level in the game and in seeing repairs and new features
> > for it.  Whose decisions and opinions deserve more weight here?
> 
> As you say, Nethack is an open source game. If you want the "official"
> version, you wait for the DevTeam. If you want to roll your own, roll
> your own. Share it with others. Maybe you'll become the next
> "standard". After all, it's happened before.


They have a pretty iron grip on officialdom.  The bug reports go to
them.  The "official" nethack site is published by them.  And after 3
years without a release, people are *still* waiting for them.  A
person can't just "roll their own" and steal the thunder.  Also,
especially against this backdrop, it would be very difficult for a
single person, as you note below, to code and test a comprehensive
update.  What is needed is a renewed, organized effort for developing
the game under a new paradigm.  And to be successful, this likely
would require the current devteam to acknowledge, and to relinquish or
participate, for the above reasons.

 
> Personally, I'm all for making a new version of our own. I'm not
> speaking idly, I'm on the PatchHack team and every now and then I make
> an effort to try to put together a new PatchHack release. But my
> experience is probably the same as that of the 'real' DevTeam -- it's a
> lot easier to knock together individual hacks than it is to combine a
> whole bunch of diverse patches and bugfixes into a single stable
> release.
> 
> It's really unfair to compare the work of the patch-writers with that
> of the DevTeam. As patch-writers we get to do the fun bit - throwing
> together a new idea and making it fly. Combining patches, standardising
> code, checking for compilation problems on multiple platforms,
> debugging -- these are the boring parts that we largely ignore.


Agreed.  One person or two won't want to do all of this.  Harnessing
the efforts of the community with a new, *productive* development
project could work.


> It is very easy to approach open source software with the same sense of
> entitlement as you do for something you have paid for, but the fact is
> that this attitude is completely unjustified, and is looking a gift
> horse in the mouth.


The devteam stopped being a gift horse a couple of years ago.
Entitlement isn't an issue; intelligent use of resources to continue
actively developing a game tradition is.

 
> So in summary:
> * The work of the DevTeam is a gift to you. Appreciate it.
> * If you want them to do the work, then you have to wait on their
> schedule.
